RetroGPU local patch 0136: VERR and VERW read no descriptor past the table's limit.

op0F00_common's VERR and VERW worked out `valid` from the selector against the
GDT's or LDT's limit and then read the descriptor's access word anyway, past
the limit too. The result was right (valid stays 0), but the read was made:
where the table ends a page early, it page-faulted, and a program that VERRs
a stray value (HDPMI's regression test IRQ0TSTP VERRs a real-mode segment,
2C5Eh, as a selector) was killed. Silicon checks the limit first and touches
no memory for a selector past it (SDM, VERR/VERW: ZF cleared). LAR and LSL
already read only when valid. Found by GLOS's memory diet (2026-11-20), whose
LDT is a sparse window since; V86TEST case Z.

--- a/src/cpu/x86_ops_pmode.h	2026-11-20 22:16:14.927692845 +0000
+++ b/src/cpu/x86_ops_pmode.h	2026-11-20 22:16:14.929167533 +0000
@@ -281,7 +281,8 @@
                 return 0; /*Null selector*/
             cpl_override = 1;
             valid        = (sel & ~7) < ((sel & 4) ? ldt.limit : gdt.limit);
-            desc         = readmemw(0, ((sel & 4) ? ldt.base : gdt.base) + (sel & ~7) + 4);
+            /* RetroGPU 0136: past the table's limit there is no descriptor to read (LAR and LSL already skip it) */
+            desc         = valid ? readmemw(0, ((sel & 4) ? ldt.base : gdt.base) + (sel & ~7) + 4) : 0;
             cpl_override = 0;
             if (cpu_state.abrt)
                 return 1;
@@ -312,7 +313,8 @@
                 return 0; /*Null selector*/
             cpl_override = 1;
             valid        = (sel & ~7) < ((sel & 4) ? ldt.limit : gdt.limit);
-            desc         = readmemw(0, ((sel & 4) ? ldt.base : gdt.base) + (sel & ~7) + 4);
+            /* RetroGPU 0136: past the table's limit there is no descriptor to read (LAR and LSL already skip it) */
+            desc         = valid ? readmemw(0, ((sel & 4) ? ldt.base : gdt.base) + (sel & ~7) + 4) : 0;
             cpl_override = 0;
             if (cpu_state.abrt)
                 return 1;
